# Fourteen Inches - a thatcher, a ridge, and another man's work under it.
# Written for the library. One person, one trade, and a decision about something found under the last man's work.

title: Fourteen Inches
start: start

== start ==
bg workshop_bench
music room_tone
A ridge is the top of a roof and it takes about a day and a half: the old straw off, the
spars checked, and fourteen inches of new laid and pegged and tight enough that a sparrow
cannot get a claw in it.
You have done four hundred of them and you can tell a good ridge from the ground by how it
sits against the light, which is a thing that is hard to explain and impossible to fake.
-> the_house

== the_house ==
The house is a long low thing at the end of a track and the ridge has gone, and the owner is a
woman of about seventy who has lived in it for forty years and has a folder of paperwork for
everything, including the last ridge, which was done nine years ago by a man called Aylward.
Nine years is short. A ridge should give you twenty-five.
-> the_news

== the_news ==
you: It wants doing, and it wants doing this month.
her: How much.
you: Twelve hundred, and I'll know more when I'm up there.
She writes it down, which she did nine years ago too, and she asks whether the last one was
done badly, and you say the honest thing you can say from the ground, which is that it has
gone sooner than it should.
-> the_up

== the_up ==
You go up on the Tuesday and strip the ridge and there it is, all the way along: the spars have
been notched too deep. Every one. He has cut into them to seat the straw, a quarter of an inch
past what you would take, and over nine years of weather the notches have opened and the whole
ridge has been sitting on a row of cuts that are not going to hold another ten years of
anything.
That is not a mistake you make with a chisel by accident. It is a mistake you make by doing it
faster.
-> the_knowing

== the_knowing ==
Two ways out of it. You can lay the new ridge on the spars as they are, and it will look right
and last about as long as the last one, and nobody will ever know from the ground. Or you can
scarf in new spars, which is a day and a half more and about four hundred pounds, and which
takes the job past what she has written in her folder.
You have Aylward's number. He is still working. He is thirty miles away and you have never met
him, and he has been doing this eleven years longer than you.
-> the_decision

== the_decision ==
The spars are under your knees and the woman is in the garden doing something to a rose and the
light is going. And she is going to ask you one question at the bottom of the ladder, and what
you say to it depends on what you do next.
* Tell her, and let her decide about the four hundred pounds. -> tell_her
* Do the spars and say nothing about the extra, and eat the difference. -> eat_it
* Ring Aylward before you say anything to anybody. -> ring_him
* Lay the ridge on what is there, and put the notches in the estimate in writing. -> write_it

== tell_her ==
You go down the ladder with your hands dirty and tell her the whole of it standing in the
garden, including what it means, which is that the four hundred pounds is not for a repair, it
is for the last job.
She listens with the secateurs in her hand and then says the thing that you have heard twice
before and never from somebody who meant it as exactly as she does.
her: He's not going to pay for it, is he.
you: He's not going to pay for it.
-> the_asked

== the_asked ==
her: Then it's mine.
She says it without any bitterness at all, which is why you remember it, and she goes in and
comes out with the folder and writes the four hundred in the margin of the nine-year-old
invoice in her own handwriting.
-> ending_hers

== eat_it ==
You scarf in nine spars over the Wednesday and the Thursday and you finish the ridge on the
Friday evening and you tell her it came to twelve hundred and it did not, and she pays you and
thanks you and you put the receipt in the van.
The van is not yours. The four hundred is four days of somebody else's money and a fortnight of
yours, and you do not tell your wife either, and the two of you have one argument about money
that winter which is not about the four hundred pounds at all and which is, and you know it while
it is happening.
-> END

== ring_him ==
He answers on the third ring and you say who you are and where you are and what you have found,
and there is a long pause with the wind on the line.
He knows. He says so in four words and then says the rest of it, which is that he was on three
jobs that month and his lad was doing the notching, and that he has thought about that ridge
about twice a year since.
-> the_man

== the_man ==
man: I'll come and look at it. Saturday, if that suits.
He comes on the Saturday from thirty miles away on his own time and he looks at nine spars for
about four minutes and then he gets his tools out of the van and works the Saturday and the
Sunday alongside you, and does not charge anybody, and does the whole row as slowly as it should
be done.
He is a better thatcher than you are. You knew that before, from his work, and you know it
differently now.
-> ending_the_man

== write_it ==
You lay the new ridge on the spars that are there, and it takes the day and a half, and it looks
right from the ground and from the road and from the top of the ladder if you do not know what to
look for.
On the estimate you write, under the total, the sentence you have been composing since Tuesday
lunchtime: *NOTE - the spars beneath this ridge are notched too deeply and will need attention.
Not included above.* She pays it without asking, because she pays everything, and she files it in
the folder, and that sentence sits in a folder in that house for however many years it is until
somebody else reads it.
-> END

== ending_hers ==
You scarf in the spars over four days with the weather holding, and the ridge goes on on the
Sunday, and it is a good one: fourteen inches, tight, sitting against the light the way it should.
It will be there when she is not. That is what a roof is, and it is not a sad thought to her - she
says so on the Sunday, standing in the garden with her hands in her cardigan, looking up.
The invoice with the four hundred in the margin is in the folder, and the file is the only place
in the world where it says who was careless and who paid for it, and she has never shown it to
anybody and never will. You have thought about Aylward's name being in that folder more often than
you have thought about the ridge.
-> END

== ending_the_man ==
He does the row and he goes home and he never mentions it again to you, and you hear his name a
year later from somebody in the trade, in a list of people who do good work, and you do not add
anything to it.
The ridge is a good one. It will be there for twenty-five years if the house is, and it will be
read by whoever next goes up on it as a piece of work by two men, because a spliced row tells you
which one did the scarfing if you know what you are looking at, and you know, and you are the only
person alive who is going to look at that roof and know whose Saturday it was.
-> END
